 It looks to me like you have used a sheet as a backdrop to mask objects or patterns that might impede the image; this is a good idea, but I find the crease lines draw the eye away from the subject almost as badly as clutter.  Also, personally, I''d have tried to keep the tail in shot. Finally, the angle of the shot is the same angle that most of us would view a dog from, and therefore, it doesn''t add to the shot.  Lying on the floor  at the animals level, can produce all sorts of interesting (and often amusing) results.  From that tirade of moans, you''d think I didn''t like the shot; on the contrary, I like the shot quite a bit; the exposure works really well and the use of all neutral colours maintains a solid balance that is almost sufficient to block all of the negative points I''ve made (8) No creases  I''d have scored the max  Keep shooting</td>', 'I love the expression on the dogs face - and don''t they just love bins It looks to me like you have used a sheet as a backdrop to mask objects or patterns that might impede the image; this is a good idea, but I find the crease lines draw the eye away from the subject almost as badly as clutter.  Also, personally, I''d have tried to keep the tail in shot. Finally, the angle of the shot is the same angle that most of us would view a dog from, and therefore, it doesn''t add to the shot.  Lying on the floor  at the animals level, can produce all sorts of interesting (and often amusing) results.  From that tirade of moans, you''d think I didn''t like the shot; on the contrary, I like the shot quite a bit; the exposure works really well and the use of all neutral colours maintains a solid balance that is almost sufficient to block all of the negative points I''ve made (8) No creases  I''d have scored the max  Keep shooting', '2002-10-09 10:01:00.000000', NULL, 1),
